WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.720
One convenience in using keywords to&nbsp;
get tasks done is the ease of getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.160
an opposite of a criterion. Take a look at this&nbsp;
query, which has person information, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.640
query has a WHERE clause to only show people&nbsp;
from Los Angeles. I will execute this query,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.320
and we see 91 rows. However, what if we wanted&nbsp;
to see everyone outside of Los Angeles? We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.880
certainly do not want to have to account for&nbsp;
every other possible city in the recordset.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.120
In most cases, we can use the word NOT to&nbsp;
get the opposite of an existing criterion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.880
Here is the tricky part, though. Watch what&nbsp;
happens when I put the word NOT in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:51.280
the equals sign and execute this query. It does&nbsp;
not work. What needs to happen is that the NOT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:57.840
needs to be in front of the entire criterion of&nbsp;
City = Los Angeles. I will put the keyword there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:08.720
execute the query, and now we see everyone&nbsp;
outside of Los Angeles. Just think opposites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:16.000
in terms of when to use the NOT keyword, and&nbsp;
you will be successful in using the NOT keyword.

